  <img src="static/llelephant.png" alt="../../static/llelephant.png" />
  <span class='pagenumber'>4</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 5 -->
<article>
  <h1>Concurrency vs. Parallelism</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>Parallelism: doing several things at the same time.</p>
<p>Concurrency: <em>seeming</em> to do several things at the
same time.</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>5</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 6 -->
<article>
  <h1>Concurrency without Parallelism</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<iframe style="width:76%; aspect-ratio:16/9; height:auto; display:block; margin:1em auto; border:0" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Pa0wXSh4I34?si=k2GjH3OSzerAA4hS" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen>
</iframe>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>6</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 7 -->
<article>
  <h1>Go and Concurrency</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>A <em>goroutine</em> is a unit of concurrency.</p>
<ul>
<li>A separate thread of execution</li>
<li>A separate program counter + stack</li>
<li>A separate &quot;finger&quot; walking through code
and executing it</li>
</ul>
<p>goroutines &gt;&gt; CPUs</p>
<p>The <em>scheduler</em> assigns goroutines to CPUs.</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>7</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 8 -->
<article>
  <h1>Go and Concurrency</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>Use the <code>go</code> keyword to start a goroutine.</p>
<p><code>go</code> is followed by a function call.</p>
<p>The goroutine runs until the function exits.</p>
<p>All goroutines run concurrently until the main
goroutine exits.</p>
  </div>
  <div class='code'><pre>
<span class='codenum'>1</span>func <defn>main</defn>() {
<span class='codenum'>2</span>   go fmt.Println(1)
<span class='codenum'>3</span>   go fmt.Println(2)
<span class='codenum'>4</span>}
</pre>
  </div>
  <details>
    <summary>
How many goroutines does this program have?    </summary>
    <div class='answer'>
<p>Three: the two that are started with <code>go</code>, and the
main goroutine.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
What does this program print?    </summary>
    <div class='answer'>
<p>Nothing: the main goroutine exits before the others have
a chance to run.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <span class='pagenumber'>8</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 9 -->
<article>
  <h1>Fix 1</h1>
  <div class='code'><pre>
<span class='codenum'>1</span>func <defn>main</defn>() {
<span class='codenum'>2</span>   go fmt.Println(1)
<span class='codenum'>3</span>   go fmt.Println(2)
<span class='codenum'>4</span>   time.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)
<span class='codenum'>5</span>}
</pre>
  </div>
<div></div>
  <div class='output'><pre>
1
2
</pre>
  </div>
<div></div>
  <div class='output'><pre>
2
1
</pre>
  </div>
  <div class='text'>
<p>goroutine execution is <em>non-deterministic</em>.</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>9</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 10 -->
<article>
  <h1>WaitGroup</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>Use a <code>sync.WaitGroup</code> to wait for
goroutines to finish.</p>
  </div>
  <div class='code'><pre>
<span class='codenum'>1</span>func <defn>main</defn>() {
<span class='codenum'>2</span>   var wg sync.WaitGroup
<span class='codenum'>3</span>   wg.Go(func() {
<span class='codenum'>4</span>      fmt.Println(1)
<span class='codenum'>5</span>   })
<span class='codenum'>6</span>   wg.Go(func() {
<span class='codenum'>7</span>      fmt.Println(2)
<span class='codenum'>8</span>   })
<span class='codenum'>9</span>   wg.Wait()
<span class='codenum'>10</span>}
</pre>
  </div>
<div></div>
  <div class='output'><pre>
1
2
</pre>
  </div>
<div></div>
  <div class='output'><pre>
2
1
</pre>
  </div>
  <div class='text'>
<p>How can we get results in order?</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>10</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 11 -->
<article>
  <h1>The Collatz Conjecture</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>Starting with an integer <em>n</em>, repeat the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>If <em>n</em> is even, divide it by 2.</li>
<li>If <em>n</em> is odd, multiply it by 3 and add 1.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
<li>4 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1</li>
<li>6 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 10 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 16 -&gt; 8 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1</li>
<li>7 -&gt; 22 -&gt; 11 -&gt; 34 -&gt; 17 -&gt; 52 -&gt; 26 -&gt; 13 -&gt; 40 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 10 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 16 -&gt; 8 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1</li>
</ul>
<p>Conjecture: All values end up at 1.</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>11</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 12 -->
<article>
  <h1>The collatz function</h1>
  <div class='code'><pre>
<comment>// collatz returns the number of steps to get to 1 from n</comment>
<comment>// using the Collatz sequence.</comment>
<span class='codenum'>1</span>func <defn>collatz</defn>(n int) int {
<span class='codenum'>2</span>   count := 0
<span class='codenum'>3</span>   for n &gt; 1 {
<span class='codenum'>4</span>      if n%2 == 0 {
<span class='codenum'>5</span>         n /= 2
<span class='codenum'>6</span>      } else {
<span class='codenum'>7</span>         n = 3*n + 1
<span class='codenum'>8</span>      }
<span class='codenum'>9</span>      count++
<span class='codenum'>10</span>   }
<span class='codenum'>11</span>   return count
<span class='codenum'>12</span>}
</pre>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>12</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 13 -->
<article>
  <h1>Computing Collatz(n)</h1>
  <div class='code'><pre>
<comment>// collatzSequential prints the first n values</comment>
<comment>// of the collatz function to w in order.</comment>
<span class='codenum'>1</span>func <defn>collatzSequential</defn>(w io.Writer, n int) {
<span class='codenum'>2</span>   for i := 1; i &lt;= n; i++ {
<span class='codenum'>3</span>      fmt.Fprintln(w, collatz(i))
<span class='codenum'>4</span>   }
<span class='codenum'>5</span>}
</pre>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>13</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 14 -->
<article>
  <h1>Exercise: Collatz concurrently</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>Start with GCLATAM26/exercises/collatz/collatz.go.</p>
<p>Write collatzConcurrent, which computes
the values of collatz concurrently, <em>but
prints the results in order</em>.</p>
<p>Hint: use a slice.</p>
<p>(You won't need synchronization.)</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>14</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 15 -->
<article>
  <h1>Questions about the exercise</h1>
  <details>
    <summary>
Does <code>collatzConcurrent(10)</code> run concurrently, or in parallel?    </summary>
    <div class='answer'>
<p>Both, on modern hardware. All modern (non-embedded) computers
have more than one CPU. The Go scheduler will assign goroutines
to CPUs until there are no more available CPUs.
(This answer assumes that <code>collatz(n)</code> runs slowly enough for small <code>n</code>)</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>
Which is faster, the sequential version or the concurrent one?    </summary>
    <div class='answer'>
<p>The sequential, by a lot.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <span class='pagenumber'>15</span>
</article>

<!-- slide 16 -->
<article>
  <h1>Concurrency and performance</h1>
  <div class='text'>
<p>The concurrent version will run <em>slower</em> than
the sequential one.</p>
<p>Concurrency has overhead.</p>
<p>To make it worthwhile, you must do a lot of work
in each goroutine.</p>
<p>See the benchmark in exercises/collatz/collatz_test.go.
128 collatz calls per goroutine does much better.</p>
  </div>
  <span class='pagenumber'>16 and last</span>
</article>

